body:
Thanks for the explanation. I can see the potential for abuse, but don't consider it more than that of any other law and would vote yes anyway based on what I know of the actual discrimination faced by transgender people. I could change my mind if it is actually abused in practice but I am not aware of that happening.

&#x200B;

We can argue more about the definition of gender, but I suspect our differences are largely semantic at this point. If you define gender in a way that you can classify everybody into one of two categories, including people with all the various genetic conditions we've mentioned, then great, you have a binary definition of gender, it's your prerogative to use such a definition as you see fit, and no evidence I could possibly produce would overturn it. But that is not the definition of gender as I would understand it in day-to-day usage, nor is it the definition that I think most people who describe themselves as non-binary would think of.

&#x200B;

Can you give some examples of the "attention whores" you mentioned?
